	"butrintTitle": "Butrint",
	"butrintSubTitle": "The Ancient Crossroads",
	"butrintRegionTitle": "Sarandë, Albania",
	"butrintArtifactCulture": "Illyrian-Roman",
	"butrintArtifactGeolocation": "Southern Albania",
	"butrintPullQuote1Top": "A Microcosm of",
	"butrintPullQuote1Bottom": "Mediterranean History",
	"butrintPullQuote2": "Butrint is a testament to the richness and diversity of a cultural landscape shaped by centuries of human settlement.",
	"butrintPullQuote2Author": "UNESCO",
	"butrintCallout1": "Butrint has been inhabited since at least the 8th century BCE and served as a Greek colony, a Roman city, a Byzantine bishopric, and a Venetian outpost.",
	"butrintCallout2": "The ancient theater, built in the 3rd century BCE, could seat approximately 1,500 spectators and is one of the best-preserved in the region.",
	"butrintVideoCaption": "\u201cButrint - Albania's UNESCO World Heritage Site.\u201d Youtube.",
	"butrintMapCaption": "Map showing location of Butrint National Park in southern Albania.",
	"butrintHistoryInfo1": "Butrint is an archaeological site in southern Albania, opposite the Greek island of Corfu. First settled by Greek colonists from Corfu in the 8th century BCE, it grew into an important city of the ancient world. According to legend, the city was founded by Trojan refugees led by Aeneas.\nThe Romans conquered Butrint in 167 BCE, and Julius Caesar later designated it a colony for veterans. Under Roman rule, the city flourished with the construction of an aqueduct, public baths, and a forum.",
	"butrintHistoryInfo2": "Butrint continued to thrive as an important ecclesiastical center in the Byzantine period. A large basilica and baptistery were built in the 6th century CE. The site changed hands many times \u2014 from Byzantines to Angevins, from Venetians to Ottomans \u2014 each leaving their architectural mark on this remarkable landscape.",
	"butrintConstructionInfo1": "The ancient theater is carved into the southern slope of the acropolis hill, taking advantage of the natural terrain. Built with local limestone in the Hellenistic period, the theater features a semicircular orchestra, tiered seating (cavea), and a stage building (skene).\nThe nearby Asclepion sanctuary, dedicated to the god of healing, features finely carved stone reliefs and columns in the Corinthian style.",
	"butrintConstructionInfo2": "The 6th-century baptistery is one of the largest early Christian structures in the Balkans. Its floor is adorned with intricate mosaics depicting animals and geometric patterns, showcasing the artistic sophistication of the era. The structure uses a circular plan with 16 granite columns supporting the interior.",
	"butrintLocationInfo1": "Butrint is located on a peninsula between the Vivari Channel and Lake Butrint in the Ksamil area of southern Albania, approximately 14 miles (23 km) south of Sarand\u00eb. The site sits within a national park encompassing wetlands, forests, and the lake itself.",
	"butrintLocationInfo2": "The strategic position of Butrint, on a narrow strait between the mainland and Corfu, made it an important stop along ancient Mediterranean trade routes. The surrounding landscape of marshes, lagoons, and the Ionian Sea coast has been crucial to the site's history as both a defensive position and a thriving port.",
	"butrint600ce": "Greek colonists from Corfu establish a settlement at Butrint, which grows into a significant trading post",
	"butrint832ce": "Butrint serves as a major Byzantine ecclesiastical center with construction of the Great Basilica",
	"butrint998ce": "The city comes under various regional powers as the Byzantine Empire's control weakens",
	"butrint1100ce": "Normans and Angevins contest control of Butrint as part of broader Mediterranean conflicts",
	"butrint1527ce": "The Venetian Republic fortifies Butrint, constructing a castle and tower to defend against Ottoman expansion",
	"butrint1535ce": "Ottoman forces gain control of the wider region, and Butrint gradually declines as a settlement",
	"butrintCollectible1Title": "Pendant",
	"butrintCollectible1Icon": "jewelry",
