	"beratTitle": "Berat",
	"beratSubTitle": "City of a Thousand Windows",
	"beratRegionTitle": "Berat, Albania",
	"beratArtifactGeolocation": "Central Albania",
	"beratPullQuote1Top": "A Rare Example of",
	"beratPullQuote1Bottom": "Well-Preserved Ottoman Architecture",
	"beratPullQuote2": "Berat bears witness to the coexistence of various religious and cultural communities down the centuries.",
	"beratPullQuote2Author": "UNESCO",
	"beratCallout1": "Berat is known for its distinctive white Ottoman houses climbing up the hillside, their many windows giving the city its famous epithet.",
	"beratCallout2": "The castle quarter (Kalaja) has been continuously inhabited for over 2,400 years and still houses a living community within its 13th-century walls.",
	"beratVideoCaption": "\u201cBerat - City of a Thousand Windows, Albania.\u201d Youtube.",
	"beratMapCaption": "Map showing location of Berat in central Albania.",
	"beratHistoryInfo1": "Berat is one of the oldest continuously inhabited cities in Albania, located along the banks of the Osum River. The castle on the hilltop has been inhabited since the 4th century BCE, when it was an important Illyrian settlement known as Antipatrea, founded by the Illyrian king Antipater.\nUnder Roman rule, the city served as a strategic military outpost along the Via Egnatia trade route.",
	"beratHistoryInfo2": "During the Byzantine period, the city was known as Pulcheriopolis and later Beligrad. The Ottomans captured Berat in 1417 and it became a major center of trade and craftsmanship.\nThe city's three historic quarters \u2014 Mangalem, Gorica, and Kalaja \u2014 preserve an extraordinary blend of Eastern and Western architecture, earning it UNESCO World Heritage status in 2008 as part of the \"Historic Centres of Berat and Gjirokast\u00ebr.\"",
	"beratConstructionInfo1": "The Berat Castle sits at 614 feet (187 m) above sea level atop a rocky hill. The current walls date primarily to the 13th century under the Despotate of Epirus, though foundations are much older. The fortress encompasses an area of about 24 acres (10 hectares) and originally had 24 towers along its walls.\nWithin the castle walls, several Byzantine churches survive, including the Church of the Holy Trinity and the Church of St. Mary of Blachernae, featuring remarkable 16th-century frescoes by the renowned painter Onufri.",
	"beratConstructionInfo2": "The Mangalem quarter below the castle is famous for its Ottoman-era houses built on the steep hillside. These traditional houses feature stone foundations, timber-framed upper stories, and large windows arranged symmetrically on whitewashed facades \u2014 the architectural signature that gives Berat its name \"City of a Thousand Windows.\"",
	"beratLocationInfo1": "Berat is situated in south-central Albania, approximately 75 miles (120 km) south of the capital Tirana. The city is built along both banks of the Osum River, at the foot of Mount Tomorr, one of Albania's most sacred mountains reaching 8,042 feet (2,416 m).",
	"beratLocationInfo2": "The Osum River canyon, stretching southeast of the city, is one of the most dramatic natural features in Albania, with vertical canyon walls reaching over 260 feet (80 m). The surrounding region has been cultivated since antiquity, producing olives, grapes, and figs in the mild Mediterranean-influenced climate.",
	"berat1850ce": "Berat experiences a cultural renaissance under the Ottoman Tanzimat reforms, with new schools and civic buildings constructed",
	"berat1921ce": "Following Albanian independence, Berat becomes an important administrative center of the new Albanian state",
	"berat1922ce": "The first modern schools in Albanian language are established in Berat, advancing the national education movement",
	"berat1926ce": "Archaeological surveys begin documenting the historic architecture of Berat's castle and Ottoman quarters",
	"berat1931ce": "Restoration work begins on the castle walls and Byzantine churches within the Kalaja quarter",
	"berat2006ce": "Berat's historic center receives preliminary recognition leading to its UNESCO World Heritage inscription in 2008",
	"beratCollectible1Title": "Engraved Horn",
	"beratCollectible1Icon": "statue",
	"beratCollectible2Title": "Fixed fan",
	"beratCollectible2Icon": "jewelry",
	"beratCollectible3Title": "Handkerchiefs (one of two)",
	"beratCollectible3Icon": "textile",

	"gjirokasterTitle": "Gjirokast\u00ebr",
	"gjirokasterSubTitle": "The Stone City",
	"gjirokasterRegionTitle": "Gjirokast\u00ebr, Albania",
	"gjirokasterArtifactCulture": "Ottoman-Albanian",
	"gjirokasterArtifactGeolocation": "Southern Albania",
	"gjirokasterPullQuote1Top": "A Rare Example of",
	"gjirokasterPullQuote1Bottom": "a Well-Preserved Ottoman Town",
	"gjirokasterPullQuote2": "Gjirokast\u00ebr is an outstanding example of a well-preserved Ottoman town, built by farmers of large estates.",
	"gjirokasterPullQuote2Author": "UNESCO",
	"gjirokasterCallout1": "The Gjirokast\u00ebr Fortress is one of the largest in the Balkans, dominating the city from a hilltop at over 1,100 feet (336 m) above sea level.",
	"gjirokasterCallout2": "The city's distinctive tower houses, known as \"kull\u00eb,\" feature slate-stone roofs and fortified architecture unique to the region, giving Gjirokast\u00ebr its name \"The Stone City.\"",
	"gjirokasterVideoCaption": "\u201cGjirokast\u00ebr - Albania's Stone City, UNESCO World Heritage.\u201d Youtube.",
	"gjirokasterMapCaption": "Map showing location of Gjirokast\u00ebr in southern Albania.",
	"gjirokasterHistoryInfo1": "Gjirokast\u00ebr is a city in southern Albania, situated in the Drino River valley. The area has been settled since at least the 1st century CE, and a fortress has stood on the hilltop since the 3rd century. The name Gjirokast\u00ebr likely derives from the medieval Greek \"Argyrokastron\" meaning \"Silver Castle.\"\nThe city rose to prominence in the 13th century as a center of the Despotate of Epirus and later became an important stronghold contested between Byzantine, Serbian, and Ottoman forces.",
	"gjirokasterHistoryInfo2": "Under Ottoman rule from 1417 onward, Gjirokast\u00ebr became the center of a large sanjak (administrative district) and thrived as a bazaar city. Wealthy landowners built the distinctive fortified tower houses that define the city's character.\nThe city was inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2005 as \"a rare example of a well-preserved Ottoman town, built by farmers of large estates.\" It is the birthplace of both former Albanian dictator Enver Hoxha and Nobel Prize-winning author Ismail Kadare.",
	"gjirokasterConstructionInfo1": "The Gjirokast\u00ebr Fortress covers an area of approximately 18 acres (7.3 hectares) on the hilltop. Expanded significantly by Ali Pasha of Ioannina in the early 19th century, the fortress features massive stone walls, a clock tower, an aqueduct, and a series of underground tunnels and chambers.\nDuring the communist era, the fortress was used as a prison and later converted into a Museum of Armaments. Today it hosts the Gjirokast\u00ebr National Folklore Festival every five years.",
	"gjirokasterConstructionInfo2": "The city's iconic tower houses (kull\u00eb) are typically three to four stories tall, built of solid stone with slate roofs. The ground floor served as a cistern and storage, the first floor for winter living, and the upper floors for summer quarters and guest reception. Defensive features include small windows on lower floors and fortified entrances.",
	"gjirokasterLocationInfo1": "Gjirokast\u00ebr is located in the Drino River valley in southern Albania, about 18 miles (29 km) from the Greek border. The city is built on the steep slopes of the Mali i Gjer\u00eb mountain range, rising from the valley floor at approximately 980 feet (300 m) to the castle at over 1,100 feet (336 m).",
	"gjirokasterLocationInfo2": "The surrounding region, known as Dropull, has been a crossroads of cultures for millennia. The Drino Valley connects the Adriatic coast with the interior Balkans, and the ancient route from the coast at Butrint passed through this valley toward the Via Egnatia.",
	"gjirokaster70ce": "Early Roman-era settlement exists in the Drino Valley region, with fortifications on the hilltop that would later become Gjirokast\u00ebr",
	"gjirokaster82ce": "The hilltop fortress is expanded as a strategic defensive position guarding the Drino Valley trade routes",
	"gjirokaster1140ce": "Argyrokastron emerges as a significant Byzantine settlement and is contested during the Norman invasions of the Balkans",
	"gjirokaster1490ce": "Under Ottoman rule, Gjirokast\u00ebr develops into a thriving bazaar town with construction of the Old Bazaar and early tower houses",
	"gjirokaster1829ce": "Ali Pasha of Ioannina undertakes major expansion of the fortress, adding fortifications, an aqueduct, and a clock tower",
	"gjirokaster1990ce": "Following the fall of communism, restoration efforts begin and Gjirokast\u00ebr receives UNESCO World Heritage status in 2005",
