	"apolloniaTitle": "Apollonia",
	"apolloniaSubTitle": "The Ancient City of Learning",
	"apolloniaRegionTitle": "Fier, Albania",
	"apolloniaArtifactCulture": "Illyrian-Greek",
	"apolloniaArtifactGeolocation": "Central Albania",
	"apolloniaPullQuote1Top": "An Admirable City",
	"apolloniaPullQuote1Bottom": "With Excellent Laws",
	"apolloniaPullQuote2": "Apollonia is considered one of the most important cities of the ancient world, a center of learning where the young Octavian studied.",
	"apolloniaPullQuote2Author": "Strabo",
	"apolloniaCallout1": "The Roman statesman and orator Cicero described Apollonia as \"a great and important city\" (magna urbs et gravis) in his work Philippics.",
	"apolloniaCallout2": "The young Octavian (later Emperor Augustus) was studying in Apollonia when he received news of Julius Caesar's assassination in 44 BCE, a pivotal moment in Roman history.",
	"apolloniaVideoCaption": "\u201cApollonia Archaeological Park - Ancient Albania.\u201d Youtube.",
	"apolloniaMapCaption": "Map showing location of Apollonia near Fier in central Albania.",
	"apolloniaHistoryInfo1": "Apollonia was an ancient Greek colony founded in 588 BCE by settlers from Corinth and Corcyra (modern Corfu) on the banks of the Aous River (modern Vjosa) in Illyria. Named after the god Apollo, the city quickly grew into one of the most important settlements on the eastern Adriatic coast.\nAt its peak, Apollonia had a population of around 60,000 and was a major stop along the Via Egnatia, the great Roman road connecting the Adriatic to Constantinople.",
	"apolloniaHistoryInfo2": "The city was renowned as a center of philosophy and learning. Numerous Roman nobles sent their sons to study there, most notably Octavian, the future Emperor Augustus. Apollonia's importance declined after the 3rd century CE when an earthquake altered the course of the Vjosa River, destroying the city's harbor and cutting off its trade routes.\nThe site was partially built over by the Byzantine-era Monastery of St. Mary, which still stands and houses an archaeological museum.",
	"apolloniaConstructionInfo1": "The archaeological park covers approximately 237 acres (96 hectares), though only a fraction has been excavated. Major structures include a monumental bouleuterion (council house) with 19 rows of stone seating, an impressive stoa (covered walkway) measuring over 260 feet (80 m) in length, and a well-preserved odeon (small theater).\nThe remains of a nymphaeum (ornamental fountain) and several temples demonstrate the city's wealth and architectural sophistication.",
	"apolloniaConstructionInfo2": "The city was built using locally quarried limestone and followed classical Greek urban planning with an agora, temples, and public buildings arranged along paved streets. The defensive walls, encircling approximately 1 square mile (2.5 sq km), were built with polygonal masonry typical of Hellenistic military architecture.\nThe 13th-century Monastery of St. Mary, built atop ancient ruins within the archaeological zone, features a beautifully decorated Byzantine church with carved stone facades.",
	"apolloniaLocationInfo1": "Apollonia is situated on a hilltop near the village of Pojani, approximately 7 miles (12 km) west of the city of Fier in central Albania. The site overlooks the Myzeqe plain and the Vjosa River valley, commanding views extending to the Adriatic Sea.",
	"apolloniaLocationInfo2": "In antiquity, the Vjosa River flowed much closer to the city, providing direct access to the Adriatic coast and enabling Apollonia to function as an important port city. The fertile surrounding plain supported agricultural production, while the hilltop position offered natural defensive advantages.",
	"apollonia700bce": "Greek colonists from Corinth and Corcyra found Apollonia in 588 BCE on Illyrian territory, establishing a settlement that would grow into a major city",
	"apollonia214bce": "Apollonia becomes a key Roman ally during the Illyrian Wars and serves as a staging ground for Roman military campaigns in the eastern Mediterranean",
	"apollonia121bce": "The city flourishes as a center of trade along the Via Egnatia, connecting Rome to its eastern provinces",
	"apollonia556ce": "An earthquake alters the course of the Vjosa River, destroying Apollonia's harbor and beginning the city's irreversible decline",
	"apollonia618ce": "The remains of the city serve as a quarry for building materials; the Byzantine Monastery of St. Mary is constructed within the ancient walls",
	"apollonia1487ce": "The site lies largely forgotten and overgrown until systematic archaeological excavations begin under Austrian and later French archaeologists in the early 20th century",
	"apolloniaCollectible1Title": "Biographies of Lian Po and Lin Xiangru",
